  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p><b>Create a CodePipeline pipeline where CodeCommit acts as the source. Build a new Docker container image in CodeBuild, push it to Amazon ECR, and then use CloudFormation to generate a new ECS task definition revision linked to the image. Finally, update the ECS service to use the latest task definition.</b> This approach is optimal for containerized deployments on AWS ECS because Amazon ECR is designed specifically as a container image registry. Matching ECS task definition to the ECR image ensures your ECS service efficiently pulls the latest container image.</p><p><i>Set up CodePipeline with a Source step using CodeCommit, then use CodeBuild to build the new artifact such as an RPM or executable file, storing it in S3. Afterward, add a Deploy step utilizing CloudFormation to create a new task definition revision that refers to the installation package in S3 and refresh the ECS service with the updated task definition.</i> is incorrect because ECS task definitions are built to work with Docker container images, not raw binaries or installers placed in S3.</p><p><i>Configure CodePipeline to use CodeCommit as the source. Employ CodeBuild to construct a new Docker image and upload it to an S3 bucket. Then apply CloudFormation in the Deploy stage to launch a new ECS Cluster linked to the S3 URL for the Docker image and update the necessary ECS service.</i> is also wrong, since Docker images should be stored in ECR (not S3) for use with ECS, and ECS cannot pull images from S3.</p><p><i>Set up a pipeline in CodePipeline using CodeCommit for sourcing. Build a new artifact such as an RPM package or executable in CodeBuild and push this directly to ECR. Use CloudFormation to create a new ECS task definition revision referencing this artifact, and update the ECS service with the new definition.</i> is incorrect, as ECR is for Docker images, not for storing RPMs or executables. Task definitions cannot consume binaries from ECR unless they're packaged as Docker images.</p><ul><li><b>Tip:</b> Always use ECR to store Docker container images when deploying to ECS.</li><li><b>Insight:</b> ECS seamlessly integrates with ECR and expects container images, not raw executables or files from S3.</li></ul>",
